A||N||11||darkknight||Fan will never turn on!||||8||
Z||000000||darkknight||02-21-2000||03:05 PM||||Hey all! first time message here! Well... my 86 SE's fan will NEVER turn on! For months I just by passed the relay box thinking it was bad but I kept blowing switches ever few weeks! ( too high of amps) I found a working relay box & wired it the same way it came from Pontiac... the temp passed 250f & the fan never turned on! I know the relay works. There's two little wires (brown & green) I ran a hot & ground to them..oddly it didnt matter which one was connected to the hot wire or the ground! This worked but even with the car turned off, it would kill my battery in a few hours! What else tells the fan to click on?  ( yes the fuse for the fan is good)  Message this or ICQ me! 39262313||||reg||8
Z||000001||87PegasusGT||02-21-2000||04:03 PM||||And you have tried replacing the temperature<BR>fan thingummy by the engine? I forget what its called though I can check.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||02-21-2000||06:50 PM||||The coolant fan is turned on via a coolant sensor switch at the engine (kind of looks like a temperature gauge sensor) when the temp reaches 230-235 degrees or if you turn on the air conditioner. Switch on the A/C and see if it works then. The fan rarely runs on my Fieros except in stop and go traffic on super hot days (unless the A/C is on).||||reg||1
Z||000003||b_b_a_d||02-22-2000||03:46 AM||||My friend had just rebuilt my V6 Auto/trans and came across a little electrical problem. There seemed to be an open circuit keeping the alternator from charging the battery.  i.e... a drain in the system.  One of the things we discovered was the radiator fan comes on with the key in acc or bulb test positions if the Air Conditioner unit is on. <BR>I don't have the magic fiero book in front of me right now since I'm at work, but you might check to see if there is another relay for the airconditioner on the same circuit.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierosound||02-22-2000||01:42 PM||||Check out what batboy said.  The biggest problem is the STUPID design GM has.  The fan switch temp sensor is set at 225F, the engine runs at 195F with stock thermostat.  So the engine actually has to OVERHEAT before the fan gets turned on!  <P>I installed a switch under the dash to manually close the ground circuit to the fan anytime I notice the temperature getting too high.  I don't have A/C.  <P>Most Japanese cars have a temp switch at the outlet side of the radiator.  When the coolant isn't being cooled enough, then the fan is switched on to cool it, and the engine gets properly cooled coolant.  If GM used this approach, Fieros wouldn't have this overheating problem.<P>Anyone done this mod???||||reg||10
Z||000005||Robert||02-22-2000||02:55 PM||||Ahhhh.... finally something I know a "little" about. If you have a V-6, ground out the wire that goes to the fan switch sensor on the engine.  It is located to the right of the water filler cap, the one with a single wire coming from it.  If the fan comes on when you turn on the key, it’s your sensor that’s bad.  If you still have no fan, and you have air conditioning, you may need a new relay.  It’s located just to the right of your left headlight.  I hope this helps,  all this accumulated knowledge  was gained by me in trying to get my own 87GT fan to work properly.   Good luck and may the force be with you.  ||||reg||3
Z||000006||theogre||02-22-2000||07:26 PM||||Some of the aftermarket fan switches for add on electriv fans could be used to add a radiator sensing fan control. Simply install the switch as directed, then use it to ground the relay control line. Souldn't take more than 1/2 hour or so to put in. You can get these anyplace that sells auxilary elctric fans. many of them are even adjustable.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||02-22-2000||08:38 PM||||Someone on the Forum last summer posted this URL about an adjustable temp sensor. I believe he installed it and thought it worked great. I've thought seriously about getting one.<P> <A HREF="http://store.summitracing.com/product.asp?ssid=61VJHCXVN2VWRY11IBX1S7PF4L&p=A6ED197F-3612-11D3-949D-080009FC5B5F" TARGET=_blank>http://store.summitracing.com/product.asp?ssid=61VJHCXVN2VWRY11IBX1S7PF4L&p=A6ED197F-3612-11D3-949D-080009FC5B5F</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phil||02-22-2000||09:42 PM||||J.C. Whitney also has a couple of different ones $15-$35||||reg||1
Z||000009||Rodney||02-22-2000||10:11 PM||||I installed an aftermarket fan on my old S-15 pick up. It was the adjustable type. A pretty good quality unit. I had it in several years. Had problems with it several times. Replaced the adjustable sending unit twice. Had the connections corrode. A fan switch in the engine is a much better way to go.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||02-23-2000||10:01 AM||||there's several way to prevent coroding. unless it's inside the switch, then it's harder.<P>an after market addon is the only way you can modify the fan control on the DIS motors. (With DIS the ECM controls the fan) So that's probly what I'll do soon. just where I'll mount it I don't know. yet. depends what switch I use.||||reg||1
Z||000011||darkknight||02-23-2000||09:09 PM||||THANKS EVERYONE!!!   I found that little spark plug looking thing , replaced it for $8 bucks & now the fan works!  (only at 235f!) oh well!  .........If there's anyone that owns a fiero in San Antonio, TX... drop me a line!||||reg||1
